WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.560
A voice call could finish while the event saying so never arrived. The pipeline did not know.

2
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:16.040
In late April a burst of calls overwhelmed event delivery. The calls happened. The pipeline lost track of what they produced.

3
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:22.610
It worked, it did not scale, and it left nothing an operator could see the next morning.

4
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:32.080
A completed launch with no completion event is a failure. The panel lists each one with four ways to resolve it.

5
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:42.340
A recovered result is fetched from the source, normalised with the worker's own logic, and replayed through the same pipeline as a live event.

6
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:52.030
A replay of an event that did arrive reuses the existing record, and a resolved case is not recovered or alerted again.

7
00:00:53.100 --> 00:01:00.560
The dashboard card carries the delivery percentage, so a quiet system and a lost event no longer look the same.

8
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:09.760
Five ways to recover a lost event where there had been a script, built in five days, with the automatic path under test.

9
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:19.200
This record measures the mechanism, not the outcome. The audit table could answer that with one query; nobody has run it yet.

10
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:27.110
Every claim on this page is pinned to a commit. Every figure spoken here is on the page underneath.
